Both are vendored BYTE-FOR-BYTE from the data repo's public contract at
conformance/exports/v1/, which is the source of truth. They originate in
the portable-exports PRD (v1.0, 2026-09-18), hand-specified alongside the
precedence table and the sweep; this copy is the pipeline's tested source
of the same expectations, and the two must never diverge.

profile-fixtures.json carries three cases added after the first vendoring,
because the published contract singles out "an ISP with any role other
than tier1_transit, INCLUDING A NULL ROLE, is residential_isp" and had no
case for the null role it highlights. isp-null-role closes that gap;
hosting-content-network and business-stub were added at the same time so
that every network_role name in the vocabulary appears in the public
conformance suite at least once, which the CSV and SQLite column
round-trips need.

Hand-specified is the whole point: every expected verdict, source list and
interval bound here was written by reading the spec, never by running
Export::Profile or Export::Project and recording what came out. Regenerating
them from the code under test would turn the acceptance oracle into a
snapshot of the current bug. If a fixture and the implementation disagree,
the implementation is wrong until the spec says otherwise.

projection-fixture.json expresses addresses as offsets from 1.0.0.0; the
tests add that origin before feeding the layers to the sweep.
